l’†\nL´aszl´o Mikl´os Lov´asz‡\nAbstract\nWe study the uniqueness of optimal solutions to extremal graph theory\nproblems. Our main result is a counterexample to the following conjecture\nof Lov´asz, which is often referred to as saying that “every extremal graph\ntheory problem has a ﬁnitely forcible optimum”: every ﬁnite feasible set of\nsubgraph density constraints can be extended further by a ﬁnite set of den-\nsity constraints such that the resulting set is satisﬁed by an asymptotically\nunique graph.\n1\nIntroduction\nMany problems in extremal graph theory do not have asymptotically unique\nsolutions. As an example, we consider the problem of minimizing the sum of the\ninduced subgraph densities of K3 and its complement. It can be shown that this\nsum is minimized by any n-vertex graph where all vertices have degrees close to\nn/2. For example, the complete bipartite graph Kn/2,n/2, the union of two (n/2)-\nvertex complete graphs, or (with high probability) an Erd˝os-R´enyi random graph\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThursday 2.10 Auditorium B (https://upcommons.upc.edu/bitstream/handle/2117/111640/AbstractBooklet.pdf)\nciteturn2search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; In particular, we will present a counterexample to the following conjecture of Lov´asz,\nwhich is often referred to as saying that “every extremal graph theory problem has a\nﬁnitely forcible optimum”: every ﬁnite feasible set of subgraph density constraints can\nMini-symposium\nEmerging methods in Extremal Combinatorics\nTuesday 3.30 Auditorium A\nUniqueness of optimal configurations in extremal\ncombinatorics\nDaniel Kr´al’\nd.kral@warwick.ac.uk\nUniversity of Warwick\n(This talk is based on joint work with Andrzej Grzesik and L´aszl´o Mikl´os Lov´asz.)\nMSC2000: 05C35\nWe study the uniqueness of optimal conﬁgurations in extremal combinatorics. An empir-\nical experience suggests that optimal solutions to extremal graph theory problems can\nbe made asymptotically unique by introducing additional constraints. Using tools from\nthe theory of graph limits, we will show that this phenomenon is not true in general.\nIn particular, we will present a counterexample to the following conjecture of Lov´asz,\nwhich is often referred to as saying that “every extremal graph theory problem has a\nﬁnitely forcible optimum”: every ﬁnite feasible set of subgraph density constraints can\nbe extended further by a ﬁnite set of density constraints such that the resulting set is\nsatisﬁed by an asymptotically unique graph.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMini-symposium (https://combinatorics.cis.strath.ac.uk/bcc2017/AbstractBooklet.pdf)\nciteturn2search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.4 years ago; In particular, we will present a counterexample to the following conjecture of Lovász, which is often referred to as saying that “every extremal graph theory problem has a finitely forcible optimum”: every finite feasible set of subgraph density constraints can be extended further by a finite set of density constraints such that the resulting set is satisfied by an asymptotically unique graph.\nMini-symposium  \nEmerging methods in Extremal Combinatorics                                                                                 Tuesday 3.30 Auditorium A\n\nUNIQUENESS OF OPTIMAL CONFIGURATIONS IN EXTREMAL COMBINATORICS\n\nDaniel Král’\n\nd.kral@warwick.ac.uk\n\nUniversity of Warwick\n\n(This talk is based on joint work with Andrzej Grzesik and László Miklós Lovász.)\n\nMSC2000: 05C35\n\nWe study the uniqueness of optimal configurations in extremal combinatorics. An empirical experience suggests that optimal solutions to extremal graph theory problems can be made asymptotically unique by introducing additional constraints. Using tools from the theory of graph limits, we will show that this phenomenon is not true in general. In particular, we will present a counterexample to the following conjecture of Lovász, which is often referred to as saying that “every extremal graph theory problem has a finitely forcible optimum”: every finite feasible set of subgraph density constraints can be extended further by a finite set of density constraints such that the resulting set is satisfied by an asymptotically unique graph.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nExtremal graph theory and finite forcibility (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1571065317301701)\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.3 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Our main result is a counterexample to the following conjecture of Lovász, which is often referred to as saying that “every extremal graph theory problem has a finitely forcible optimum”: every finite feasible set of subgraph density constraints can be extended further by a finite set of density constraints such that the resulting set is satisfied by an asymptotically unique graph.\n\n## Electronic Notes in Discrete Mathematics\n\nVolume 61, August 2017, Pages 541-547\n\n# Extremal graph theory and finite forcibility\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.endm.2017.07.005Get rights and content\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe study the uniqueness of optimal solutions to extremal graph theory problems. Our main result is a counterexample to the following conjecture of Lovász, which is often referred to as saying that “every extremal graph theory problem has a finitely forcible optimum”: every finite feasible set of subgraph density constraints can be extended further by a finite set of density constraints such that the resulting set is satisfied by an asymptotically unique graph.\n\n\n## References (17)\n\n  * L. Lovász et al.\n\n### Generalized quasirandom graphs\n\n### J. Combin. Theory Ser. B\n\n(2008)\n\n  * L. Lovász et al.\n\n### Finitely forcible graphons\n\n### J. Combin. Theory Ser. B\n\n(2011)\n\n  * L. Lovász et al.\n\n### Limits of dense graph sequences\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nElusive extremal graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.01141)\nciteturn2academia16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.4 years ago; This statement is often referred to as saying that `every extremal graph theory problem has a finitely forcible optimum'. We present a counterexample to the conjecture.\nTitle: Elusive extremal graphs\nAuthors: Andrzej Grzesik, Daniel Král', László Miklós Lovász\nDate: Tue Jul  3 13:15:09 2018\n\nWe study the uniqueness of optimal solutions to extremal graph theory problems. Lovasz conjectured that every finite feasible set of subgraph density constraints can be extended further by a finite set of density constraints so that the resulting set is satisfied by an asymptotically unique graph. This statement is often referred to as saying that `every extremal graph theory problem has a finitely forcible optimum'. We present a counterexample to the conjecture. Our techniques also extend to a more general setting involving other types of constraints.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRegular complex Hadamard matrices of order 7,8 (https://hexagonmath.org/pdf/2611.00079v1)\nciteturn2search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: yesterday; *with \\(B\\) partial Butson and \\(V^{(s)}\\) integer matrices, valid as a regular Hadamard family for all \\(q_s\\in\\mathbb T\\). ... ### 1.4  The regularity conjecture\nThe enveloping defect \\(d_{\\mathrm{env}}=\\dim_{\\mathbb R}\\widetilde T_H\\) counts all infinitesimal phase deformations. Removing the \\(K+N-1\\) directions coming from row and column scalings gives the dephased defect\n\\[\nd_{\\mathrm{dep}}=d_{\\mathrm{env}}-(K+N-1).\n\\]\nThis bounds the dimension of dephased families, but infinitesimal deformations need not extend to actual families [12, 31, 32].\n\n**Theorem 1.4 (Affine realization and finite cover).** *For fixed \\(K,N\\), the regular locus is a finite union of tori translated by torsion points. Each regular matrix has a representation*\n\\[\nH_{ij}=B_{ij}\\prod_{s=1}^{d}q_s^{V^{(s)}_{ij}},\n\\]\n*with \\(B\\) partial Butson and \\(V^{(s)}\\) integer matrices, valid as a regular Hadamard family for all \\(q_s\\in\\mathbb T\\). Chosen prime cycles and dephasing can be preserved throughout. Regular Butson matrices are dense in every component; every isolated dephased regular matrix, and every dephased representative of an isolated regular equivalence class, is Butson.*\n\n**Proof.** Choose a cycle decomposition of every row quotient. Coordinates not constrained by these equations can vary freely around a circle. Returning to the original coordinates expresses the entries using integer powers of the free circle parameters and fixed roots of unity.\n\nThere are only finitely many choices of cycle blocks and their orderings. For each choice, the solutions form a finite union of closed tori, so the regular locus is a finite union of closed tori. In the Smith normal form coordinates, each constrained coordinate is rational and the free coordinates vary on circles. Joining a point to a rational point by a straight line in the free phase coordinates gives the regular path\n\\[\nH(t)_{ij}=e(x_{0,ij}+t(x_{ij}-x_{0,ij})),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R,\n\\]\nwhere \\(x_0\\) is rational. Rational values of the free parameters are dense, so Butson matrices are dense in each torus. If a dephased regular matrix is isolated, it must therefore be Butson. The same argument applies to a dephased representative of an isolated equivalence class, since its dephased orbit is finite. \\(\\square\\)\n\nDifferent cycle decompositions can give intersecting, nonmaximal families. The theorem preserves the chosen cycles; classification still requires completion and equivalence analysis.\n\n### 1.4  The regularity conjecture\n\n**Conjecture 1.5 (Regularity).** *Every square Butson matrix is regular.*\n\nLam–Leung proves this at levels with at most 2 prime divisors